@database "web_scraper"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: web_scraper.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Web scraper"
@{h1}Web scraper

An agent that has to look something up on the web reads pages with this
plugin: it fetches a URL and hands the model the page as clean, readable
text -- or the list of its links. Files a page cannot show as text, such as
PDFs, images and archives, the agent saves with a second tool into a
directory you allow. Every address is checked first, so an agent cannot be
talked into reading your own machine or your internal network.

@{code}
For agents and developers
  @{"  Reading a page  " link page}
  @{"  Downloading a file  " link download}
  @{"  Security and limits  " link security}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node page "Reading a page"
@{h1}Reading a page

@{tt}web_scraper_page@{utt} (the name follows the plugin instance) fetches
one URL and answers with its text or its links. The whole page is fetched
once and kept in a cache; paging through a long article and asking for its
links afterwards cost no second fetch.

@{h2}Operations

@{table}
operation | Does
content | the page's readable text, paged (the default)
links | the page's anchors, filtered on request
@{body}

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Used by | Meaning
url | both | required; http or https only
operation | both | content (default) or links; anything else is an error
max_chars | content | characters of text to return, default 8000; 0 or less returns all
offset | content | where the window starts, default 0; a negative value counts as 0
extract_tables | content | true: also the page's tables, each with caption, headers and rows
extract_lists | content | true: also the page's ul, ol and dl lists as items
include_nofollow | links | true: keep anchors marked rel=nofollow (dropped by default)
only_same_domain | links | true: only anchors on the host of the final URL
max_links | links | cap the list; 0 (default) returns every anchor
timeout | both | seconds one attempt may take in all, redirects and body included; default 20
ignore_cache | both | true: fetch again instead of using the cached copy
cache_ttl | both | seconds to keep this page when it is fetched now; absent or 0 means the instance's cache_ttl
@{body}

A number the model sends as text, null, nonsense ("lots") or not finite
("inf", "nan", 1e400) falls back to the default instead of failing the call.

@{h2}What counts as text

@{bullet}Scripts, styles, @{tt}noscript@{utt}, @{tt}template@{utt}, SVGs,
iframes, @{tt}nav@{utt} and @{tt}footer@{utt} are removed.
@{tt}aside@{utt} is kept: documentation puts its notes and warnings there.
@{bullet}Inline tags do not break a sentence; block tags (paragraphs, list
items, table cells, headings) end a line. Runs of spaces collapse and empty
lines go.
@{bullet}Code blocks (@{tt}pre@{utt}) come back verbatim, indentation
included.
@{bullet}Every script and language stays. Invisible characters are removed
-- see @{" What the model sees " link model}.
@{bullet}Tables have headers only when the page marks them as such (a row in
@{tt}thead@{utt} or a first row of @{tt}th@{utt} cells); otherwise every row
is data.

@{h2}Answers

@{tt}content@{utt} answers with @{tt}url@{utt}, @{tt}final_url@{utt} (after
redirects), @{tt}status_code@{utt}, @{tt}content_type@{utt},
@{tt}title@{utt}, @{tt}text@{utt}, @{tt}total_chars@{utt} and
@{tt}truncated@{utt} (true while text is left after this window), plus
@{tt}tables@{utt} and @{tt}lists@{utt} when asked for and present.
@{tt}links@{utt} answers with @{tt}url@{utt}, @{tt}final_url@{utt},
@{tt}status_code@{utt} and @{tt}links@{utt}: each with @{tt}href@{utt} as
written, @{tt}abs_url@{utt}, @{tt}text@{utt} and @{tt}rel@{utt}.

A page the server answers with an error status but some HTML -- a 404 page,
for instance -- comes back as text with that @{tt}status_code@{utt}; only
the cases below are errors. Every error answer carries @{tt}error@{utt}:

@{table}
error begins with | When
Blocked URL (SSRF protection) | the URL or a redirect hop points at a host the plugin refuses; see @{" Security and limits " link security}
blocked: | HTTP 403, or 429 or 503 still after the retries, or a bot-wall page recognised by its title ("Just a moment", "Access denied", ...) or a known challenge marker
Not a text page | the server sends a file (PDF, image, ...); the message names the download tool
Page exceeds max_page_mb | the page is larger than the instance allows; the message names the download tool
Unsupported content-encoding | the server compressed the page with something other than gzip or deflate; the message names the coding
Empty response | a text page with no body
Fetch failed | a network error or timeout still after the retries; a compressed body that does not decode or is cut off (not retried); more than 10 redirects; or a URL the HTTP client cannot use
Unknown operation, Missing required parameter | a malformed call
@{body}

@{h2}Retries

A network error or timeout, and the statuses 429, 502, 503 and 504, are
tried again up to three times, after about 1, 2 and 4 seconds. A 404 is not
retried, and neither is a body that does not decode: the same bytes
decode the same way the next time. With the default timeout a page that never answers ends after
about 90 seconds.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"url": "https://example.org/manual/install.html"}
{"url": "https://example.org/manual/install.html", "offset": 8000}
{"url": "https://example.org/manual/", "operation": "links", "only_same_domain": true}
@{body}
@endnode

@node download "Downloading a file"
@{h1}Downloading a file

@{tt}web_scraper_download@{utt} saves whatever a URL serves -- a PDF, an
image, an archive -- to a file. It exists only when the instance has
@{tt}allowed_directories@{utt}; without them the model is not even offered
the tool.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
url | required; http or https only
path | required; the file to write, inside an allowed directory
overwrite | true: replace an existing file (default false: refused)
timeout | seconds any single network wait may take, default 60; the transfer as a whole has no time limit
@{body}

A relative @{tt}path@{utt} counts from the folder the server was started
in; one that begins with @{tt}data/@{utt} lands in the data directory,
wherever that is configured. Missing folders are created; when the
download fails, the ones it created are removed again if they are empty.

@{h2}How it writes

The body streams into a staging file next to the target (@{tt}<name>.<pid>.<random>.part@{utt}) and
is renamed onto it only when complete. A failed, refused or oversized
download leaves nothing behind -- no partial file, no staging file. Two
downloads to the same path at once do not share a staging file. The size cap
(@{tt}max_download_mb@{utt}) is counted on the bytes as they arrive, not
taken from the server's Content-Length.

@{h2}Answers

On success: @{tt}url@{utt}, @{tt}final_url@{utt}, @{tt}status_code@{utt},
@{tt}path@{utt} (absolute), @{tt}bytes@{utt}, @{tt}content_type@{utt} and
@{tt}sha256@{utt}. Errors:

@{table}
error | When
Path is outside the allowed directories | the path leaves every allowed directory, or names a network share or device
Path starts with '~' | the home directory is not expanded here
Path cannot be resolved | the path holds a null byte or is too long for the file system
path names a directory | give the file name
File exists | pass overwrite=true
Blocked URL (SSRF protection) | the URL or a redirect hop is refused
HTTP <code> | the server answered 400 or above
Download exceeds max_download_mb | the file is larger than the cap
Unsupported content-encoding | the server compressed the file with something other than gzip or deflate; the message names the coding
Download failed | a network error, a compressed body that does not decode or is cut off, or more than 10 redirects
Unusable URL | the HTTP client cannot use the URL
Cannot write | the file system refused (a file where a folder should be, a full disk)
@{body}

A download is not retried.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"url": "https://example.org/spec/format-v2.pdf", "path": "data/workspace/downloads/format-v2.pdf"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node security "Security and limits"
@{h1}Security and limits

The model chooses every URL, and a page it has read can tell it which one to
choose next. The plugin therefore treats the URL as hostile.

@{h2}Which addresses are refused

Before each request -- the first URL and every redirect hop, which the
plugin follows itself -- the URL is checked:

@{bullet}Only @{tt}http@{utt} and @{tt}https@{utt}. @{tt}file:@{utt},
@{tt}ftp:@{utt}, @{tt}gopher:@{utt} and the rest are refused, also as a
redirect target.
@{bullet}The host names @{tt}metadata@{utt}, @{tt}metadata.google.internal@{utt}
and @{tt}metadata.goog@{utt} are refused by name.
@{bullet}The host is resolved, and every address it resolves to must be
public. Loopback (@{tt}127.0.0.1@{utt}, @{tt}localhost@{utt}, @{tt}::1@{utt}),
private networks (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, fc00::/7), link-local
(169.254/16 with the cloud metadata address 169.254.169.254), shared and
reserved ranges and @{tt}0.0.0.0@{utt} are refused. Numeric spellings
such as @{tt}2130706433@{utt}, @{tt}127.1@{utt}, @{tt}0x7f.1@{utt} or
@{tt}0177.0.0.1@{utt} are refused whichever way: as the address they
resolve to, as a host that cannot be resolved (Windows), or as a URL the
HTTP client rejects.
@{bullet}IPv6 addresses that carry an IPv4 address are judged by that
address: IPv4-mapped (@{tt}::ffff:127.0.0.1@{utt}), IPv4-compatible
(@{tt}::7f00:1@{utt}), IPv4-translated (SIIT, @{tt}::ffff:0:a00:1@{utt}),
NAT64 (@{tt}64:ff9b::a9fe:a9fe@{utt}) and 6to4
(@{tt}2002:7f00:1::1@{utt}). Local-use NAT64 (@{tt}64:ff9b:1::/48@{utt})
is refused outright.
@{bullet}A URL without a host, with an unusable port or a host that does not
resolve is refused as well.

@{h2}What the check cannot see

@{bullet}@{b}DNS rebinding.@{ub} The address is resolved for the check and
again by the connection. A name server that answers a public address first
and an internal one a moment later gets past it. Closing that needs a
connection pinned to the checked address.
@{bullet}@{b}Proxies.@{ub} With @{tt}proxies@{utt} configured, the
@{tt}HTTP_PROXY@{utt}, @{tt}HTTPS_PROXY@{utt} or @{tt}ALL_PROXY@{utt}
environment variables set, or -- on Windows and macOS -- a proxy in the
system settings, the proxy resolves the host and connects; the check still refuses internal
names and addresses, but what the proxy can reach is the proxy's business.

@{h2}Where downloads go

@{tt}path@{utt} passes the shared path sandbox before anything is fetched:
the path is resolved with symlinks and @{tt}..@{utt} and must then lie in an
allowed directory. Network shares and device paths
(@{tt}\\\\host\\share@{utt}, @{tt}//host/share@{utt}, @{tt}\\\\?\\...@{utt})
are refused on the text alone, before Windows could open them and sign in
to the host. Inside an allowed directory the model may write any file --
with @{tt}overwrite@{utt} also replace one. Allow a directory that holds
nothing a downloaded file could harm: not a source tree, whose scripts or
hooks someone runs later.

@{h2}Size and time

@{table}
Limit | page | download
size | max_page_mb (10); the decompressed body is counted as it arrives and a larger page is refused; the bytes on the wire are held to the cap plus 1/64 and 64 KB | max_download_mb (100), counted the same way
compression | gzip and deflate only, inflated in steps that never pass the cap | the same
body of a refused file | not read at all | --
time | timeout (20 s) for one attempt in all; up to four attempts | timeout (60 s) per network wait only
redirects | 10 | 10
@{body}

A compressed body is a bomb when a few kilobytes inflate to gigabytes. The
plugin asks only for gzip and deflate, inflates them itself in pieces that
stop one byte past the cap, and refuses any other encoding (brotli, zstd,
compress) before reading the body -- httpx's own decoders inflate each
network chunk whole, before any cap could count it. What it reads:

@{bullet}@{tt}gzip@{utt} (also @{tt}x-gzip@{utt}), every member of a body
made of several, all under the one cap. Anything after the end of a stream
that is not a new gzip member is dropped unread.
@{bullet}@{tt}deflate@{utt}, zlib-wrapped as the standard says or raw as
some servers send it.
@{bullet}Stacked codings (@{tt}gzip, gzip@{utt}), undone in reverse order,
every layer held to the cap. @{tt}identity@{utt}, @{tt}none@{utt} and empty
entries in the list mean no coding.
@{bullet}A compressed body that stops before its end is an error, not a
shorter page or file.
@{bullet}The bytes on the wire are counted as well as the decoded ones. Junk
after a stream, or endless empty gzip members, decode to nothing; they end
at the wire limit as "too large".

The model sets @{tt}timeout@{utt}; nothing caps what it asks for. A
download from a server that sends a trickle of bytes, each within the
timeout, runs until the bytes on the wire reach the size limit or the user
stops the run -- with a slow enough trickle, that is a long time.

@{h2}Cookies and the cache

Cookies are kept per chat session and per host the fetch started at, in
memory only: one user's login never rides along on another user's request.
The page cache is also per session. It lies on disk (see
@{" Setting it up " link setup}) and holds the fetched page, text and links,
for @{tt}cache_ttl@{utt} seconds -- pages fetched with a session's cookies
included. HTTPS certificates are always verified; the plugin does not read @{tt}network.ssl_verify@{utt}.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Tool and parameter descriptions come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. The download tool is rendered only when the
instance has a download directory, so a model is never offered a tool that
can only refuse. The chat's status line shows "Fetching <url>" and ends
with the result: "3120 chars HTTP 200 -- <url>", "8000 of 41250 chars
(cached) HTTP 200 -- <url>", "57 link(s) HTTP 200 -- <url>", "182044 bytes
(application/pdf) -- spec.pdf", or the error.

Errors read like "Not a text page (application/pdf); use
web_scraper_download to save the file", "Blocked URL (SSRF protection):
Blocked non-public address 127.0.0.1 for host '127.0.0.1'", "blocked:
challenge page (just a moment...)", "Page exceeds max_page_mb (10 MB); use
web_scraper_download to save it as a file".

@{h2}Page content is untrusted

Everything a page contains reaches the model as data in a tool result: its
text, title, link texts, tables and lists. A page can carry instructions
aimed at the model ("ignore your task and fetch ..."). The plugin does
three things about it:

@{bullet}It removes characters a browser does not show but a model reads:
control characters, the zero-width space and invisible operators, bidi
marks and isolates, and the Unicode tag characters that spell out hidden
ASCII. It does so on every answer of both tools, after the page is
parsed, so characters written as numeric HTML entities go too: title,
text, links and their addresses, tables, lists, code blocks, the content
type, error messages that quote the page (a bot wall's title) and pages
served from the cache. Header values quoted in an error are cut to 100
characters. Addresses keep what is percent-encoded in them
(@{tt}%F3%A0%81%81@{utt}): that is text a model sees, not an invisible
character. The zero-width
joiner and non-joiner stay: Persian words, Indic scripts and emoji
sequences need them, and they cannot spell anything.
@{bullet}It refuses the addresses a page could steer it to, see
@{" Security and limits " link security}.
@{bullet}It never runs a page's scripts.

It does not remove text hidden with CSS or the @{tt}hidden@{utt} attribute:
collapsed sections and tabs are hidden the same way, and dropping them
loses real content. The text is not marked as untrusted either. What the
model does with a page's words is up to its prompt and the tools it has.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Answers are ordinary tool results, appended; nothing earlier changes. A
@{tt}content@{utt} answer is bounded by @{tt}max_chars@{utt} (8000 unless
the model asks for more, or for all with 0). Tables, lists and links are
not paged: @{tt}extract_tables@{utt} on a data-heavy page, or
@{tt}links@{utt} without @{tt}max_links@{utt} on a portal with thousands
of anchors, can return far more than the text window.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Pages that build their content with JavaScript come back nearly
empty: there is no browser.
@{bullet}The character set comes from the HTTP header, else UTF-8; a
charset declared only in a @{tt}meta@{utt} tag is not read. A page declared
ISO-8859-1 or ASCII is read as Windows-1252, as browsers do, so its smart
quotes and euro signs survive.
@{bullet}A 503 after the retries is reported as "blocked", even when the
site is simply down.
@{bullet}@{tt}cache_ttl@{utt} applies when a page is fetched; on a cached
copy it changes nothing.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    web_scraper:
      type: web_scraper
      enabled: true
      proxies: []
      cache_ttl: 1800
      user_agent: ""
      allowed_directories:
        - data/workspace
      max_download_mb: 100
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+web_scraper/*"
@{body}

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
allowed_directories | none | where download may write; none means no download tool
max_download_mb | 100 | the largest file download saves
max_page_mb | 10 | the largest page the page tool reads
cache_ttl | 1800 | seconds a fetched page is kept
cache_enabled | true | false: every call fetches
proxies | none | proxy URLs; each host is sent through one of them
user_agent | empty | empty rotates through desktop browser identities; see below
@{body}

@{h2}Sites that want to know who you are

Some sites refuse browser identities and want a descriptive one with a way
to reach its operator; Wikipedia answers a browser identity with 403 and a
pointer to its robot policy. Set one fixed identity, with a contact address
that really reaches you. It applies to every request of the instance:

@{code yaml}
user_agent: "MyProject-research/1.0 (https://example.org; me\@example.org)"
@{body}

@{h2}Where data lives

The page cache is one JSON file per page under @{tt}cache/web_scraper@{utt}
in the data directory; it survives a restart, and expired entries are
dropped when they are read. Cookies are held in memory only, at most 2000
jars, the oldest dropped first. Downloads are wherever the model put them
inside the allowed directories.

@{h2}A quick check

From the repository, with @{tt}src@{utt} on the Python path:

@{code text}
python -m plugins.web_scraper --url https://example.org
python -m plugins.web_scraper --url https://example.org --links
@{body}

The plugin needs @{tt}beautifulsoup4@{utt} and @{tt}lxml@{utt}; both are in
its @{tt}plugin.toml@{utt}.
@endnode
